        <header class="article-header">
          <div class="article-meta"><span class="path">The Semantic Ratchet</span><time datetime="2026-10-16">2026-10-16</time></div>
          <h1>Let the compiler<br>remember<span class="title-dot">.</span></h1>
          <div class="header-foot"><span>{{ site.author }}</span><span>Type-safe design <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Kotlin</span></div>
        </header>

        <section class="prose" id="remember">
          <p class="lead">When a program establishes a fact, its representation must preserve that fact wherever later behavior needs it.</p>
          <p>That is the <strong>Semantic Ratchet</strong>. A parser produces a stronger value. A consumer accepts that value. The next operation starts with the knowledge that the previous operation established.</p>
          <p>This principle matters for humans and AI agents alike. A comment asks the next author to remember a rule. A constrained type lets the compiler reject a call that breaks the rule.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="prose" id="state-space">
          <h2><span class="section-number">01</span>Reduce the state space</h2>
          <p>Consider this interface:</p>
          <figure class="code-window">
            <figcaption class="code-titlebar"><span class="window-square" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>receipt.kt</span><span class="code-language">Kotlin</span></figcaption>
            <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Receipt function with string arguments"><code><span class="kw">fun</span> <span class="fn">sendReceipt</span>(customerId: <span class="type">String</span>, email: <span class="type">String</span>)</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>A caller can reverse the arguments or pass an unrelated string. Those calls remain type-correct.</p>
          <p>Now give each concept a distinct type:</p>
          <figure class="code-window">
            <figcaption class="code-titlebar"><span class="window-square" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>receipt.kt</span><span class="code-language">Kotlin</span></figcaption>
            <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Receipt function with distinct domain types"><code><span class="kw">fun</span> <span class="fn">sendReceipt</span>(
    customerId: <span class="type">CustomerId</span>,
    email: <span class="type">EmailAddress</span>,
)</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>The compiler rejects swapped arguments. It does not prove that the caller chose the correct customer or address.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="prose" id="preserve">
          <h2><span class="section-number">02</span>Preserve the fact</h2>
          <p>A page size must lie between 1 and 100. The parser accepts an external integer. Success carries a <code>PageSize</code>. Rejection carries the supplied integer in a distinct case.</p>
          <figure class="flow-figure">
            <div class="figure-heading"><span>Fig. 01</span><span>Parse → preserve → consume</span></div>
            <div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="An untrusted integer enters PageSize.parse. Accepted values carry PageSize to firstPage. OutOfRange returns to the caller.">
              <div class="flow-node raw"><span>External input</span><strong>Int</strong><small>untrusted</small></div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
              <div class="flow-node parser"><span>Establish the fact</span><strong>PageSize.parse</strong><small>1 ≤ n ≤ 100</small></div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
              <div class="flow-node accepted"><span>Carry the fact</span><strong>PageSize</strong><small>→ firstPage</small></div>
              <div class="rejection"><span aria-hidden="true">└─</span> OutOfRange → caller</div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The parser establishes the range. The consumer accepts the value that preserves it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="prose" id="types">
          <h2><span class="section-number">03</span>Carry it forward</h2>
          <figure class="code-window">
            <figcaption class="code-titlebar"><span class="window-square" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>page.kt</span><span class="code-language">Kotlin</span></figcaption>
            <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="A consumer accepts the constrained PageSize"><code><span class="kw">fun</span> &lt;T&gt; <span class="fn">firstPage</span>(items: <span class="type">List</span>&lt;T&gt;, size: <span class="type">PageSize</span>): <span class="type">List</span>&lt;T&gt; =
    size.<span class="fn">takeFrom</span>(items)</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p><code>firstPage(items, 0)</code> fails to compile. <code>PageSize(0)</code> also fails because the constructor is private.</p>
          <p>The compiler does not prove that the parser implements the intended range. That implementation still needs review and executable evidence.</p>
